repl_step — implementation plan
================================

status: done (2026-07-09)

references I1..I5 (plans/repl_list). pass-through ("step" => keys
["repl", "isar_text"]); THE hot-path tool — everything the model does
in a proof goes through it, so the description and the failure
semantics carry the most weight of any wave-1 tool. also the tool for
which I1's async property matters most: a step can legitimately run
for seconds (timeout default 10s) and must not block concurrent
repl_list/repl_state calls.

advertised metadata (the builtin table row)
-------------------------------------------

name          repl_step
description   "Apply one Isar command to a REPL and print the
              resulting proof state. Examples: 'lemma \"True\"',
              'by simp', 'definition ...'. Do not send 'theory'
              headers — the theory context was set by repl_init.
              IMPORTANT: if a step FAILS (error result), the REPL
              state is UNCHANGED — do NOT call repl_back to undo a
              failed step. Steps are subject to the REPL's timeout
              (default 10s, see repl_timeout); a timed-out step is a
              failed step."
              (source: mcp_server.py step :318 verbatim, plus the
              timeout sentence from ir.ML exec_text.)
inputSchema   {"type": "object",
               "properties": {
                 "repl": {"type": "string"},
                 "isar_text": {"type": "string"}},
               "required": ["repl", "isar_text"]}
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": false, "idempotentHint": false,
               "openWorldHint": false}
outputSchema  none.

name/argument mapping across the layers
---------------------------------------

  mcp client edge   tools/call "repl_step",
                    {"repl": "T", "isar_text": "by simp"}
  scala builtin row backend.ir("step", [(repl,T), (isar_text,"by simp")])
  ml dispatcher     "step" -> Ir.step (get "repl") (get "isar_text")
  engine            ir.ML:481 — with_claim Op_Step; exec_text under
                    the repl's timeout; appends the step; out
                    (pretty_state of the new state)

