assumptions and how to test them
--------------------------------

T1  isar text survives the trip byte-clean: newlines, quotes,
    Isabelle symbols (\<Longrightarrow>), unicode. (encoding half
    already covered by the I2 round-trip tests; this asserts the
    engine end.)
    test [ml unit]: step with a multi-line lemma statement containing
    \<Longrightarrow> -> ok; text output returns it verbatim
    (with plans/repl_text).
T2  failure atomicity — the description's central promise: a failing
    step leaves step count and last state unchanged, and the repl is
    NOT left claimed.
    test [ml unit]: init, step "lemma True", step "garbage_tactic" ->
    error; show -> still 1 step; step "by simp" -> ok (repl usable,
    claim released).
T3  a timed-out step behaves like a failed step.
    test [ml unit]: timeout T 1; step a sleeping/looping method ->
    error mentioning timeout; state unchanged; restore timeout.
T4  proof-state output: a successful step inside a proof prints the
    goal state (what the model steers by), not just "ok".
    test [ml unit]: after "lemma \"x + y = y + (x::nat)\"" the output
    contains "goal" and the subgoal text (already partially covered
    by the build-time self-test's s2/o2 assertions).
T5  async under load (I1): a slow step on repl A does not delay
    repl_list or a step on repl B.
    test [bridge]: fire slow step on A, then ir("repls") and a fast
    step on B; both return before A joins. (this is the spec's async
    case; also listed as I1's test — this tool is where it bites.)
T6  full chain.
    test [e2e, test_agentic.py]: init -> step lemma -> step by simp
    -> text shows both lines.

